    "author_association": "NONE",
    "source_id": "issue:4297",
    "repository": "streamlit/streamlit",
    "body": "### Summary\r\n\r\nWhen using session_state on the backend and browsing the app, if the websocket close between the front and the back, on websocket re establishment session_state is lost.\r\n \r\n### Steps to reproduce\r\nHaving a proxy between the front and the back, setting a connection timeout with the value of your choice, websocket will close after this value and session_state for this particular user will be lost.\r\n\r\nIt happened to me because I have my streamlit app running on GCP Cloud Run behind a Load Balancer. \r\nI can set the timeout to maximum 60 minutes but best behaviour would be that streamlit backend is able to recover session_state of a particular user on websocket re establishment.\r\n\r\n**Expected behavior:**\r\n\r\nUser will see no change on websocket close -> backend is able to retrieve session_state for the user on websocket close/reopen.\r\n\r\n**Actual behavior:**\r\n\r\nUser losts his work saved in session_state on websocket close.\r\n\r\n### Debug info\r\n\r\n- Streamlit version: 1.3.1\r\n- Python version: 3.7.10\r\n- Using Conda [X]\r\n\r\n### Additional information\r\nWould love to have a workaround I could apply now :)\r\nAnd subsidiary question would be: is this planed to store session_state on an external datastore (redis?).\r\nIt seems that it would allow running multiple backends behind a LB to handle more users simultaneously.",
